      {
        "heading": "Culture lives in the occasion",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The wording matters, but so does the moment in which it is spoken. The lemon saying could be a gentle reminder to ourselves. Directed sharply at someone asking for help, it could become a rebuff. Knowing the words does not settle whether their use is generous, fair or appropriate.",
          "Learning an expression therefore involves learning a relationship: who can say it to whom, in what tone, and with what possible effect. The hiding feet can prompt shared laughter; the stubborn wood can end a conversation that needed to continue. A translation can explain the picture. A conversation helps us understand its place.",
          "This is what I mean by culture embedded in language. The fruit, the fire, the family word and the wood that answers back give us more than vocabulary. They offer ways of making experience shareable. A saying lets a speaker approach a difficult thought indirectly; a listener may recognise the point, laugh at it, resist it, or turn it back on the speaker.",
          "In Odia Cultural Identity and Pride, I argued that pride grows through familiarity—through names we can use, stories we understand and skills we can practise. Dhaga give that thought a modest, practical beginning. We can learn one expression well enough to explain it, recognise a fitting occasion, and ask when its advice might fail."
