        "text": "Odia Bibhaba belongs within a wider effort to keep Odia literature available to readers and listeners. Recite World, Odia Wikisource and Odia Virtual Academy offer different ways to encounter that literature, hear its words and return to its texts. Recite World brings listening into this picture. Its own account identifies it as a programme of the Bhubaneswar-based Udaygiri Foundation Trust, intended to make Odia literary heritage accessible through listening and reading. It gives particular importance to dramatisation, pronunciation and encounters with unfamiliar words. Its page for Fakir Mohan Senapati’s Patent Medicine brings together an author introduction, a story description inviting the visitor to listen, and a PDF link. The title connects with the Gopal Chhotray adaptation catalogued by Odia Bibhaba. A reader can therefore approach the same literary work through a story presentation and the record of its transformation for the stage. This adds another dimension to preservation: a voice can draw attention to rhythm, dialogue and humour, inviting someone to return to the written work. A recorded reading is also an interpretation. Its performer and recording deserve their own credits alongside the writer and the edition used. Odia Wikisource shows how preservation can continue beyond the scan. Its page for Fakir Mohan Senapati’s Rebati presents the story as readable Odia text, with editing history and links to reading formats. A Wikimedia account published in January 2015 describes work on Bhagabata that included converting legacy-font text, proofreading and formatting. This was work on the usability and accuracy of the words, as well as their availability. The project also makes unfinished work visible. Its scan-linked index for the 1901 Barnabodha has numbered pages and a status indicating that the file needs checking. Such signals help readers distinguish a text that is available from one whose checking is complete. Odia Virtual Academy provides another entrance to Odia material. Its stated remit includes books, journals, manuscripts and archival records. Its novel catalogue offers discovery by title and author and lists works such as Antima Jatra and Matimatala. This is useful to a reader who knows a writer’s name but needs help finding a work. A catalogue listing, however, should not be mistaken for a guarantee that every linked file is complete or searchable. These collections can overlap. A scan may travel between a digitising group, a hosting institution and a volunteer transcription project. Recognising each contribution gives a clearer account of the work than treating every website as an entirely separate rescue of the same book."
