      {
        "heading": "Two roofs, two spaces",
        "text": "Behind the torana are the shrine tower and the jagamohana, the hall in front of the shrine. Mukteswar’s hall has a stepped, pyramidal roof. The taller tower rises in a curving outline. These forms belong to the rekha and pidha vocabulary of Odisha’s temple architecture: the curving tower over the sanctum and the receding horizontal tiers over the hall. The distinction is also one of use. The sanctum houses the deity and is the focus of ritual worship; the hall provides a space before it where people can gather or wait. Once you recognise the pair, the building becomes a sequence of spaces rather than a single carved silhouette. Bikashrd’s side-view photograph below makes the contrast particularly clear. Follow the horizontal roof courses on the left, then the rising lines of the tower on the right. This is a way to read the photograph and the exterior; it does not depend on entering the shrine."
