        "text": "Dalma brings lentils and vegetables together, but the finishing spices matter too. The city’s published recipe cooks the lentils and vegetables, adds a separate tempering in ghee, then finishes with roasted cumin and dried chilli ground to a powder. It lists either toor or moong dal and vegetables such as brinjal, yam and cauliflower. This gives you two things to notice: what has softened together in the pot, and what has been added towards the end. Ask which vegetables were used and how the cook finishes the dalma. A recipe can explain a method without becoming a rule for every household or kitchen."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bhubaneswar at the table · Pakhala: ask what is in the bowl",
        "text": "Pakhala starts with cooked rice and water, but that description leaves room for different preparations. The city’s recipe includes curd, ginger, green chilli and lemon, with an optional mustard-and-cumin tempering. The name alone therefore does not tell you whether the bowl contains dairy or how it has been seasoned. Ask whether the kitchen is serving a fresh or fermented preparation, and which accompaniments come with it. Let that answer guide the rest of the order. Pakhala need not be treated as one more bowl alongside an already complete meal; you can make it the centre of lunch and choose the sides around it."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bhubaneswar at the table · Chhena poda: a sweet that keeps changing",
        "text": "Chhena poda is a baked chhena sweet. In a 2021 account, Bhubaneswar chef Michael describes making a chocolate version for the city’s .FEST celebration. His recipe mixes chocolate into the chhena before baking. It is a specific example of a cook adapting an Odia sweet, rather than proof that every version follows one fixed formula. The same account places the sweet’s beginnings in Nayagarh and retells the story of Sudarshana Sahoo leaving sweetened chhena in a warm oven. Read that as the origin story the chef recounts, not an independently established invention date. The documented modern adaptation is a different kind of evidence from the retrospective story. The photograph here was taken in Puri. Its browned edges and pale cut surfaces give you something concrete to compare when you encounter the dish yourself. The fruit and garnish belong to this photographed serving; they do not define how chhena poda must be presented."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bhubaneswar at the table · Korakhai and the life of Old Town",
        "text": "Old Town gives the meal a more local connection. The city’s Streets of Ekamra account places korakhai among sweets offered to deities in the neighbourhood and connects temple food with the cooks of Ananta Vasudev. Food here is part of worship and skilled work as well as something a visitor might buy. In her 2016 report, Sheela Pattanayak describes korakhai made with khai, sugar or jaggery, coconut and cardamom. Temple sevak Trinath Badu tells her that designated confectioner families prepare the Lingaraj offering. Shop owner Subash Sahu describes customers buying the sweet for themselves as well as for offerings. These are named accounts from that report, not new interviews. The distinction matters when you buy a packet: a sweet associated with a temple is not automatically a consecrated offering. Ask who made it and how it is intended to be used. The report preserves a moment in the city’s food trade; its shop details and prices cannot establish what is available today."
      }
