        "text": "The museum’s 2019 catalogue describes terracotta roof tiles from Barpalli in Bargarh, decorated with animals including monkeys, squirrels and frogs. A tile keeps out the weather. A creature on that tile invites a second look. Imagine noticing it on the way home: something useful has made space for delight. These are examples documented in 2019; ask which pieces are currently on display."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A sari can carry an equation",
        "text": "In its 2019 catalogue, the museum includes mathematical equations woven using single ikat. It also discusses yarn preparation and cloth carrying verses from the Gita Govinda. Pattern begins before the finished cloth. What must a weaver plan while looking at yarn rather than a completed image? Carry that question into a textile gallery, and read the labels before linking any particular piece to the catalogue examples."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Follow a painted line",
        "text": "Start at the centre of this photographed panel. Follow a curved line towards the edge, then find where a figure interrupts the pattern. The photographer identifies it as a chariot front panel. The picture records a display in July 2018; it does not establish the age of the object or identify its maker. A current museum label is the place to begin those questions. Try a small looking game with a friend: each choose a detail, without pointing. Describe it until the other person finds it. A museum visit can be a conversation as well as a collection of names."
      },
