    "practical": [
      {
        "heading": "The story on the cloth",
        "text": "Look beside the brush in this photograph. A face takes shape inside an arch; patterned borders lead the eye from one figure to the next. Before trying to name the whole scene, choose one small detail and follow it. Pattachitra carries Jagannath imagery and narrative scenes on prepared cloth. A sequence of pictures can guide the eye through a story. Ask a willing artist where to begin reading one work: which gesture connects two figures, and what would you miss if you looked only at the centre?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Before the picture, the surface",
        "text": "A painting begins before the first figure appears. Cloth needs a ground that can hold colour and a fine brush line. The traditional sequence below gives you three things to notice; ask the artist how their own materials and method differ."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A craft that found another future",
        "text": "Raghurajpur’s painting tradition has depended on finding buyers as well as passing on skill. A 2005 Orissa Review account describes a decline in the painters’ fortunes and a mid-century revival. Citing dance historian Ileana Citaristi, it links new opportunities around 1953 to Halina Zealey’s involvement. This is a retrospective account, not a full record of the village’s changing livelihoods. That history makes a useful question for a maker today: who taught them, and who do they make work for? Let their answer describe their own experience."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The painter’s son who became a dancer",
        "text": "Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra was born in Raghurajpur. His father, Chintamani Mohapatra, was a chitrakara painter; Kelucharan trained in Gotipua under Balabhadra Sahu. His early life connects the village’s painting and performance traditions through a particular family and teacher. Gotipua is performed by boys in female attire. Kelucharan’s later career took him into theatre and collaboration with other artists. The connection between painting and dance is worth exploring, but neither a village nor a single life explains the whole history of Odissi. Compare a painted hand with a dancer’s gesture. What changes when one position moves into the next? This is a way to look, not a claim about the artist’s intention. Arrange a performance separately from a workshop visit."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A doorway into someone’s working day",
        "text": "In his account of a 2019 visit, Biswa Prakash describes artists explaining their work and painting on coconuts and bottles as well as cloth. A curved object gives a painter a different space to work with. Ask how the artist chooses a surface and arranges the scene around it."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Meet a maker, with permission",
        "text": "Begin with a greeting and ask whether this is a good time. Let the artist explain one piece at their own pace. If you would like a demonstration, agree its length and fee before it starts. Request permission separately for photographs of people, artwork and the home. For a dance visit, confirm the invitation with the school or organiser. Where children take part, respect schooling, supervision and permission for photographs."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bring a story home",
        "text": "Choose a piece you want to live with, not just carry away. Ask for the maker’s preferred name, materials, price, packing and care advice, and keep a receipt. If work is commissioned, agree the design, delivery and payment terms in writing. Buying a painting does not itself give permission to reproduce the artwork. Record the story only as its teller wishes it to be shared."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Leave room for the visit",
        "text": "Puri can be a base for a separately arranged village visit. Arrival options include Puri railway station and Bhubaneswar’s airport. Agree the final transfer, exact meeting point and return with your host or driver; published distances are not journey times. October to February is the seasonal suggestion in the national visitor guide. Check the forecast and choose an appointment that suits the maker, with time for a pause. Plan your main meal in Puri unless a host has agreed otherwise. The coastal food guide offers a starting point; ask about the day’s menu and ingredients when you choose where to eat."
      }
    ],
