        "text": "A boat draws the eye towards the horizon. Jobra brings it back to the work that makes a journey possible. The Odisha State Maritime Museum occupies the former Jobra Workshop in Cuttack. The museum connects the workshop’s establishment with the aftermath of Odisha’s famine of 1866 and reports a foundation plaque dated 1 January 1869. Begin with that nineteenth-century setting: water, engineering and the need to make things work."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Follow the work through the room",
        "text": "The museum describes a workshop that built boats, fabricated sluice gates and supplied canal and river works. Its account names lathes, drills and cutters, a pulley system powered first by steam and later electricity, and a rail-mounted crane for heavy materials. Look at the photograph before reading on. Let your eye follow the rails, then rise towards the wheels overhead. In the gallery, a label can turn an unfamiliar shape into a task you understand. Ask which equipment is currently on view. Choose one machine and try three questions: what went into it, what moved it, and what came out? There is no need to learn every name to leave with a new way of looking."
