        "text": "In The Question That Brought Me Here, I wrote about encountering poetry before identifying it as poetry: through a melody, a memorable voice, or a story that enters family conversation. Dhaga invite a similar encounter. A listener can enjoy the sound or recognise the situation before asking what kind of literature it is. Consider this expression: ବଣ ପୋଡ଼ିଲେ ଦୁନିଆ ଜାଣେ, ମନ ପୋଡ଼ିଲେ କେହୀ ନ ଜାଣେ\n\nWhen the forest burns, the world knows; when the heart burns, no one knows. The two halves hold the same shape. ବଣ and ମନ draw forest and inner life close through sound; the repeated burning carries us from a visible blaze to pain that may remain unseen. The second half changes what we understand from the first. “No one knows” gives the feeling its force, without requiring us to believe that care or understanding is impossible. Here is compression, parallelism and metaphor working together. A private feeling becomes imaginable through a public scene. We do not need to turn every saying into a formal poem to recognise its literary power. The joke and the judgement often arrive together. Three expressions show how much can happen between the picture we first recognise and the thought we carry away. A family word becomes a practical question ନାହିଁ ମାମୁଁ ଠଉଁ କଣା ମାମୁ ଭଲ\n\nA one-eyed maternal uncle is better than no maternal uncle. The repeated word mamu places the comparison within a family. We are invited to imagine someone to whom we might turn, rather than an abstract quantity of help. The sentence moves quickly from absence to possibility: something useful may still be available even when it is not everything we hoped for. Its blunt comparison can carry a teasing, rueful voice. Beneath that voice is a question we meet repeatedly: do we wait for the ideal, or make thoughtful use of what is within reach? A small borrowed room may let a reading group begin. It need not be the room they hope eventually to have. The saying makes room for a beginning; it does not require us to accept every bad arrangement. The bodily comparison also carries a prejudice we should recognise. Disability is not a measure of a person's worth. We can understand the practical thought while choosing a kinder image when we speak to someone. Praharaj's Purnnachandra Odia Bhashakosha records the saying and explains it through the care an uncle might offer a nephew. It also places other-language equivalents beside it. This is a printed witness within the dictionary's 1931–1940 publication series, not an account of who first made the saying. The feet outside the hiding place ଲୁଚିଛି ନା ଗୋଡ ଦିଇଟା ଦିଶୁଛି!\n\nAre you hiding? Your two feet are showing! The scene is already a joke. Someone thinks they have disappeared; the listener can still see their feet. The words for hiding and showing echo one another while pulling in opposite directions. The contradiction makes the line easy to hear and remember. It can describe a transparent excuse, an unsuccessful pretence or a secret whose clues remain in view. Imagine someone concealing a surprise parcel while its bright wrapping sticks out of the bag. The humour comes from the distance between what the person believes they have achieved and what everyone else can see. That distance gives the joke its deeper question: what do we fail to notice about ourselves? We may attend carefully to our intentions and scarcely at all to the signs we leave behind. The saying offers a quick way to puncture that confidence. Used affectionately, it lets someone laugh at the gap; used harshly, it can humiliate. Nor does a suspicious-looking clue, by itself, establish guilt. A March 2012 Aahwaan editorial uses a related form while discussing literature and politics, recalling it as an expression heard in childhood. Here we can see a saying doing work in literary discussion: a visible bodily image becomes a way to question a public appearance. The recollection does not date its first use. The wood that answers back ମୋତେ ଯେତେ ମାଠିବୁ ମାଠ ମୁହିଁ ସେଇ ଦରପଡ଼ା କାଠ\n\nPolish me as much as you like; I remain that half-burnt wood. This time the object speaks through a person. Someone's attempt to polish or improve the speaker meets a comic refusal: keep trying; I am still myself. The repeated polishing and the sound of ମାଠ against କାଠ make the answer feel compact and conclusive. There may be affection in such a reply, or exasperation, or a joke at one's own expense. Its meaning changes with the occasion. Is the speaker resisting needless pressure to become fashionable? Or using a familiar line to escape advice worth hearing? The same witty answer can protect independence or shelter stubbornness. That is where the life of the saying becomes interesting. It gives us a voice, but it cannot make our judgement for us. We can enjoy the stubborn wood and still ask whether this is a moment to stand firm or to learn. Remembering the line is one thing; recognising the difference is another. These are proverbs and idiomatic expressions, not interchangeable rules or necessarily formal poems. Their literary force comes through different means: a family comparison, a comic scene, a speaking object, an answering sound. Each makes a thought easier to carry, while leaving us responsible for how we use it."
