        "text": "21. Refusing to be polished into something else ମୋତେ ଯେତେ ମାଠିବୁ ମାଠ ମୁହିଁ ସେଇ ଦରପଡ଼ା କାଠ Polish me as much as you like; I remain that half-burnt wood. ଅର୍ଥ: ଯେତେ ବୁଝାଇଲେ ବା ବଦଳାଇବାକୁ ଚେଷ୍ଟା କଲେ ମଧ୍ୟ ନିଜେ ସେପରି ରହିବି ବୋଲି କହିବା। The wood answers back in the first person. Repeated polishing meets repeated refusal, and ମାଠ against କାଠ makes the answer snap shut. It can be comic self-recognition or stubborn defiance; it is a voice in a conversation, not a verdict that people cannot learn. Imagined occasion: A friend who always arrives with too many books jokes about remaining unchanged after another plea to pack lightly. 22. There will be another winter ଏକା ମାଘକେ ଶୀତ ଯାଏନାହିଁ। Cold does not finish with a single Magha. ଅର୍ଥ: ଆଜିର ଦରକାର ସରିଗଲେ ମଧ୍ୟ ଆଗକୁ ପୁଣି ଦରକାର ପଡ଼ିପାରେ। What sounds like a remark about weather can become a reminder that we may need each other again. Spoken warmly, it encourages continuing care; spoken sharply, it can sound like a warning. Imagined occasion: After receiving help with a difficult task, someone remembers to be available when the helper needs a hand months later. 23. Each place has its own ways ଦେଶକେ ଫାଙ୍କ୍, ନଈକେ ବାଙ୍କ୍ Each place has its particular ways; each river has its bends. ଅର୍ଥ: ସବୁ ଅଞ୍ଚଳର ରୀତି ଏକା ପରି ନୁହେଁ; ନଈର ବାଙ୍କ ପରି ପ୍ରତ୍ୟେକ ସ୍ଥାନର ନିଜସ୍ୱ ଢଙ୍ଗ ଅଛି। ଏହା ସ୍ଥାନୀୟ ଚଳଣି ବୁଝିବାକୁ କହେ, ସବୁ ରୀତିକୁ ନିର୍ବିଚାରରେ ମାନିନେବାକୁ ନୁହେଁ। The bends belong to the river; local ways belong to a place. The compact pairing gives difference a shape we can recognise. Learning how things are done elsewhere begins with attention, without requiring us to accept every practice uncritically. Imagined occasion: At a gathering in another town, a visitor asks how guests usually take part before assuming they match home. 24. Distance makes things look better ଦୂର ପର୍ବତ ସୁନ୍ଦର; ଦୂର ବନ୍ଧୁ ସୁନ୍ଦର A distant mountain is beautiful; a distant friend is beautiful. ଅର୍ଥ: ଦୂରରୁ ଦେଖିଲେ ପାହାଡ଼ ଓ ବନ୍ଧୁ—ଉଭୟଙ୍କ ଦୁର୍ବଳତା ଲୁଚିଯାଇପାରେ। ନିକଟରୁ ଜାଣିଲେ ଚିତ୍ରଟି ବଦଳିପାରେ। Both may look simpler before we encounter their rough edges. The parallel invites affection with clearer sight, not the conclusion that friendship is better kept remote. Imagined occasion: Someone envies a friend’s apparently effortless life until they hear about the work behind it. 25. Do not laugh at another’s turn ଉପର ବରଡ଼ା ଖସୁଛି,\nମଝି ବରଡ଼ା ହସୁଛି \nତଳ ବରଡ଼ା କହୁଛି,\nମୋ ଦିନ କାଳ ଯାଉଛିରେ ଭାଇ ତୋ ଦିନକାଳ ଆସୁଛି ॥ The upper leaf falls; the middle leaf laughs; the lower leaf says, “My time is passing, brother; your time is coming.” ଅର୍ଥ: ଅନ୍ୟର ଅବସ୍ଥା ବଦଳିବା ଦେଖି ହସିବା ପୂର୍ବରୁ ନିଜ ପାଳି କଥା ଭାବିବାକୁ ଏହା ମନେ ପକାଏ। ସମୟ କାହା ପାଇଁ ଅଟକି ରହେନାହିଁ। The leaves fall, laugh and speak. This editorial reading hears a warning in their exchange: another person’s turn is not separate from our own passage through time. The changing voices make the reflection memorable without settling a botanical explanation or an original occasion. Imagined occasion: A young colleague laughs at an older person’s difficulty with a new tool; another invites them to remember being a beginner."
