  {
    "id": "reading:stories/maritime-memory",
    "title": "Boita: trade, craft and the crossing of a sea",
    "kind": "Reading",
    "area": "Odisha and the sea",
    "summary": "What did Odisha’s merchants bring home? How were their boats built? And could an older migration lie behind the maritime story? Follow the objects, the craft and the competing explanations.",
    "href": "/stories/maritime-memory/",
    "checked": "2026-10-17",
    "practical": [
      {
        "heading": "Begin with a question",
        "text": "A little boat carrying a lamp is a powerful way to remember a voyage. To understand the world behind it, we also need to look at a broken bowl, a fitted plank and the changing wind. Each reveals a different part of the crossing—and each belongs to its own time."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Begin with the boat we remember",
        "text": "Boita Bandana sets small boats carrying lamps afloat; Bali Jatra in Cuttack commemorates Odisha’s maritime connections with Southeast Asia. They bring the ocean voyage into the scale of a household and a riverbank. But remembrance, a trading port and a prehistoric migration are different kinds of history. We cannot give them one departure date. Nor can we assume that “boita” names a single ship design used unchanged across centuries. The most revealing story begins when we ask what each piece of evidence actually preserves."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "What does your family place in its little boat, and what meaning does that act have for you? A household memory need not speak for every household."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The merchants were part of an exchange",
        "text": "The trade was more than taking local produce away to sell. At Khalkattapatna, beside the Kushabhadra estuary, Rudra Prasad Behera’s 2016 ceramic study describes imported porcelain and celadon bowls and plates alongside locally made pottery. Chinese coins were also reported. These objects make the receiving shore visible: goods reached Odisha as well as leaving it. Manufactured goods belong in the picture too. Book II, chapter XI of the Arthashastra lists Kalinga among regions whose cotton fabrics were highly valued. This is evidence for the reputation of a product, not a surviving bill of lading showing that a particular cloth sailed to Bali. A textile reference and a medieval ceramic assemblage cannot simply be combined into the cargo of one timeless Sadhaba ship. “Exchange” also needs care. Imported objects establish circulation; they do not tell us whether a transaction used barter, money, credit or a gift. A coin at a port does not settle the payment method for every sale. Goods could pass through several merchants and ports. The evidence here supports a connected trading network, while individual contracts, prices, profits and return cargoes still need documentation."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "Could this object have reached the port through an intermediary? What would distinguish a direct voyage from a chain of exchanges?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "An anchor connects two Indian coasts",
        "text": "A broken stone anchor from Manikapatna, on the Chilika coast, adds a connection beyond the familiar Odisha–Bali pairing. In 2022, Sila Tripati and colleagues examined its rock through petrography, chemical analysis and isotopes. Basalt near Palitana in Gujarat emerged as the most likely source. The rock gives the story a material starting point. It does not identify the ship’s owner or crew, prove where the anchor was made, or draw every stage of its journey. Even the tools of voyaging could travel through networks. To recover one voyage, we would need evidence that joins its vessel, date, people and cargo."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "Which is being traced here: the rock, the anchor, or the vessel? Those journeys may be related without being identical."
      },
      {
        "heading": "People crossed the sea as well as objects",
        "text": "I. Wayan Ardika and Peter Bellwood’s Sembiran research connects Indian traders with Bali through archaeological finds. That India–Bali connection matters, but the published abstract does not assign every Indian object to Odisha. Bali had its own communities and histories; it was a participant in exchange, not an empty destination waiting for visitors. A different kind of evidence comes from Angkor Borei in Cambodia. A study published in 2022 found South Asian ancestry in a child buried at the Vat Komnou cemetery, directly dated to 78–234 CE. This is evidence that contact could involve families and ancestry, not just objects moving between markets. The study does not identify an Odishan parent, a merchant occupation or the boat that made the crossing. Keep the distinction: goods can travel without their makers, and ideas can be learned without a population moving wholesale. A shared motif, a religious practice or a similar-sounding word needs its own dated history. The strongest account of cultural exchange asks who encountered whom and how something changed in the receiving community, rather than treating resemblance as a certificate of origin."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "What evidence would show people moving, rather than an object or an idea passing from hand to hand?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "There was no single boat for every water",
        "text": "The evidence discussed here includes no complete excavated Sadhaba ship from which to draw an exact construction plan. Medieval images offer fragments of the equipment. A Ratnagiri Tārā relief, assigned a late-eighth-century date in the scholarship discussed by Tripati and colleagues, depicts a mast, supporting stays and a steering oar. No sail is shown. The scene concerns rescue from maritime danger; it is not a shipyard drawing. Later craft studies let us examine actual construction traditions. Tripati’s 2015 chapter distinguishes the nauka of Chilika, the padhua associated with surf work, and the patia of the northern coast. In its nauka account, planks are fastened with wooden dowels and iron nails and seams are caulked with cotton. The padhua description instead has stitched planks, a flat bottom and no keel or frames. It is paddled and steered with a large stern oar. That padhua was used for beach-seine fishing and, historically, transfer between shore and larger vessels. Such work explains why a small coastal boat belongs in maritime history without turning it into an ocean-going Bali trader. These are later documented practices: they help us understand the range of solutions, but do not prove which arrangement an ancient merchant’s ship used."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "Was a boat intended to cross a lagoon, negotiate surf, work an estuary or sail offshore? Ask about its task before judging its shape."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A plank becomes part of a hull",
        "text": "Swarup Bhattacharyya’s patia study follows builders along the Odisha–West Bengal coast. The owner specifies a length; other dimensions follow in proportion. Planks are heated and bent with a wooden device called merry kath. The hull takes shape before its internal floor and side timbers are fitted: this is shell construction. The patia combines reverse-clinker, carvel and clinker joints in different parts of the hull—different ways of overlapping planks or meeting their edges. Builders check symmetry with string, fasten the wood with nails and seal seams with cotton. Bhattacharyya also records fisherman Arjun Behera’s explanation that lighter arjun timber makes smaller boats easier to drag ashore. The account connects a material choice to the work of landing a boat. These details put skill back into the maritime story. The person fitting a joint must judge a curve, a gap and a material, then correct the work. Practical knowledge deserves attention in its own right. It need not be dressed up as a lost mathematical formula, or projected unchanged into antiquity, to be impressive."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "Look at the seam between two boards: how is it joined, sealed and supported? That small detail can tell you more than the size of the boat."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The cargo changes the boat",
        "text": "A floating boat displaces water whose weight balances the total weight it carries: hull, equipment, people and cargo. Add a load and the hull sits deeper. This is the modern buoyancy explanation. It helps us understand the builder’s problem without claiming that an ancient crew used the equations in a modern physics textbook. Consider the consequence. More cargo leaves less height between the water and the edge of an open hull. A boat that floats in calm water is not thereby ready for waves. Weight distribution, the shape of the immersed hull, sound joints and the ability to keep water out all matter. These are engineering questions for any reconstruction; without a securely identified hull and loading arrangement, an exact ancient carrying capacity would be guesswork. The merchant’s wish to carry more and the crew’s need to preserve a margin against swamping therefore belong in the same conversation. So do food, drinking water and repair equipment: necessities have weight too. We have not recovered a Sadhaba provisioning list or a measured safety margin, and should not supply one from imagination."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "When a model is labelled with a cargo capacity, what measurements and loading assumptions support that number?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The return journey had a season",
        "text": "A voyage needed a way home. Tripati and L. N. Raut’s 2006 study reconstructs an outward season using the northeast monsoon and a return season using the southwest monsoon. They relate wind and circulation patterns to historical accounts and festivals. This is an explanation of how seasonal travel could work, not a recovered timetable proving that every ship departed on one festival morning. Wind and current do different jobs: a sail responds to air, while a current carries the surrounding water. A helpful seasonal wind does not make every coastal approach or strait favourable. Waiting, choosing an approach and understanding local depth belong beside the long crossing. Knowing which way to head is different from knowing whether a loaded vessel can clear a shallow entrance. Kartika Purnima also invites a tidal question. Around full and new moons, the tidal range is generally larger: high waters are higher and low waters lower. That is the physical principle explained by NOAA. It does not establish a universal rule that all heavy ships launched on Kartika’s full moon. The local channel, timing, weather and depth still matter. The ritual calendar is evidence of remembrance; a historical launch rule requires its own evidence."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "What did a navigator need to know for the whole crossing, and what could only be learned about a particular harbour entrance?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Could an older migration lie behind the story?",
        "text": "There is a serious maritime migration hypothesis worth examining. In 2019, linguists Felix Rau and Paul Sidwell proposed that a small group speaking an ancestor of the Munda languages reached the Mahanadi delta from Southeast Asia around 3,500–4,000 years ago. In their model, interaction with people already living there helped form Proto-Munda, which later spread inland. Their case combines language geography, reconstructed agricultural vocabulary and changes in word structure that they interpret as evidence of language contact. Their conference slides also acknowledge that the proposed early archaeological connection is more tentative and less securely dated than later maritime contacts. This is a model to test, not a named voyage. The authors leave the Southeast Asian departure point uncertain. Genetics supplies another kind of evidence. Kai Tätte and colleagues’ 2019 study of 102 Munda speakers estimated South Asian–Southeast Asian admixture between roughly 2,000 and 3,800 years ago for the sampled populations. “Admixture” means ancestry from previously differentiated populations coming together. A genetic estimate does not identify a landing beach, ship or festival. Present-day populations used as statistical comparisons are not automatically the actual ancient migrants. The wider population history remains debated. Debashree Tagore and colleagues’ 2021 analysis proposed much older shared ancestry between Indian and Malaysian Austroasiatic-speaking populations, followed by separation and later population changes. It also acknowledged missing ancient genomes from central and northeastern India. Its estimated population split and the 2019 study’s admixture dates describe different processes; they should not be presented as two dates for the same voyage. Linguist John Peterson, in an author-posted study, separately challenges the restricted prehistoric distribution assumed for Munda, arguing for a wider spread into the eastern Gangetic plains. None of this establishes that Odisha’s tribal communities arrived together on Sadhaba trading boats. “Munda languages” is a linguistic grouping, not a synonym for every tribal community in Odisha. The proposed prehistoric crossing, the much later objects at medieval ports, and the living Boita commemoration occupy different timelines. A connection between them is an important research question; it has not been demonstrated by the evidence reviewed here."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A question to carry",
        "text": "Which question is a study answering: language history, ancestry, a migration route, a trading voyage or the history of a ritual? A strong connection must bridge those questions with evidence."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bring three questions to Jobra",
        "text": "Let this reading change how you look at a museum. Our Jobra guide is a place to begin planning; these questions are an invitation to notice, not a promise about today’s exhibits. What am I looking at: an original object, a model, or a later reconstruction? What date and place does its label give—and whose work made it possible? Which detail would I explain to a friend, and which question would I bring home?"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to read this story",
        "text": "Ancient finds, later boatbuilding accounts and living traditions belong to different periods. The notes identify the evidence and its limits."
      }
    ],
