      {
        "heading": "Before the dome, there were words",
        "text": "The inscriptions are in Prakrit, written in Brahmi script. Dhauli preserves Major Rock Edicts I–X and XIV, with two Separate Rock Edicts. This is not the complete set of fourteen found elsewhere. When reading a translation, first check which edict and which site it represents. Major Rock Edict XIII, often quoted for its account of the Kalinga War, is absent at Dhauli. In Ven. S. Dhammika’s collection, the rendering of XIII follows the Kalsi version. Words about the war should not be mistaken for words carved on this particular rock."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The people behind the proclamation",
        "text": "“All men are my children.” In Ven. S. Dhammika’s English rendering of the first Kalinga Rock Edict, Ashoka addresses the judicial officers at Tosali. The edict warns against unjust imprisonment and harsh treatment. It is an instruction to officials, not evidence that they always obeyed. That distinction matters: a proclamation tells us what the ruler wanted to declare, while the lives of the people governed by it are harder to recover. What would that promise have meant to someone waiting for a judgment? Keep the question beside the words."
