        "heading": "What the city keeps",
        "text": "At Barabati, begin with what remains: a gateway, a moat and the mound associated with the palace, beside the Mahanadi. The district’s description recalls a nine-storeyed palace, but the building itself is gone. That difference matters when looking at the site. The gateway gives you stonework to examine; a picture of the lost palace needs evidence beyond the surviving entrance. A piece of Tarakasi asks you to look on a much smaller scale. In the process documented by NID Bengaluru, thicker silver wire forms the outline and finer wire is curled, folded or twisted into the spaces within it. Joining and polishing turn these separate parts into a finished piece. Follow one curve from the outer frame into the pattern: the open spaces are as much a part of the design as the metal. Two of Cuttack’s museums offer another useful comparison. The Netaji Birth Place Museum occupies Subhas Chandra Bose’s ancestral home and follows his life through photographs, letters and other material. A letter can tell you who was addressing whom, and when. Give one document that attention before moving on to the larger account of his political life. At Jobra, the workshop is part of the story. The Maritime Museum describes a place that made boats, sluice gates and equipment for canals and river works. Its workshop display includes machines and a crane that moved heavy material along rails. These objects direct attention to the work behind water transport: cutting, shaping, lifting and repair. They belong to a later industrial history, not a surviving ancient boita shipyard. Choose one of these threads for the day. If you arrange a craft visit, ask who designed a particular piece and who made it; those may be different people. If you choose a museum, leave time to read a few labels carefully. A food stop and an unhurried return are enough to complete the plan.",
