  {
    "id": "experience:chilika-mangalajodi-birding",
    "title": "Watch the wetlands at Mangalajodi",
    "kind": "Experience",
    "area": "Mangalajodi",
    "summary": "Watch a bird long enough to describe what it is doing. The name can come next.",
    "href": "/visit/experiences/mangalajodi-birdwatching/",
    "checked": "2026-09-29",
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "Incredible India · Mangalajodi",
        "url": "https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/odisha/bhubaneswar/mangalajodi"
      },
      {
        "title": "American Birding Association · Code of Birding Ethics",
        "url": "https://www.aba.org/aba-code-of-birding-ethics/"
      }
    ],
    "practical": [
      {
        "heading": "Choose a season, then a patch of water",
        "text": "Mangalajodi’s shallow waters and reed beds offer places for birds to feed and shelter. November to February is the winter period highlighted by Incredible India for migratory birds. Use that as a planning window, not a promise of a particular species or number. Once you are there, ask your guide to choose a place where the boat can pause without disturbing birds. Begin with one small area: an open channel, the edge of the reeds or a patch of exposed ground. Notice which parts the birds are using before trying to name them all."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Describe before you identify",
        "text": "Try a simple exercise. Pick one bird you can see comfortably and note three things: its outline, the shape of its bill and how it moves. Is it walking, swimming, standing still or reaching into the water? Describe only what you can actually see. Then ask your guide which features support an identification. If two birds look alike, ask what separates them. Write down a local name as well, if one is offered, and the spelling your guide prefers. Leave a question mark when the view is too brief. A useful note might have spaces for the date, place, shape, action and possible name. Keep an observation such as “bill dipped into the water” separate from an explanation such as “feeding on fish” unless you saw the prey or have another reason for the conclusion. These are suggestions for your notebook, not observations recorded on a visit."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Keep the birds’ attention elsewhere",
        "text": "Give feeding and resting birds room. The American Birding Association’s code asks birders to avoid stress, minimise habitat disturbance and use restraint with recordings and photography. These are general birding principles; follow the local guide’s access instructions and the rules that apply at Mangalajodi. For this outing, try listening without playing calls back to attract a bird. Use binoculars or a longer view instead of asking the boat to press closer. If the guide advises moving on, let an unfinished observation stay unfinished. Let others in the boat have time to look. Describe a bird’s position with a landmark that everyone can find, then wait while they locate it. Ask before recording your guide’s explanation, and credit them if they are comfortable being named."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Arrange the pause as well as the trip",
        "text": "Before booking, agree the meeting point, length of the outing, price and return arrangements. Ask whether binoculars are provided or whether you should bring your own. Discuss boarding, suitable lifejackets, shade, toilets and a seated break according to your group’s needs. Check what happens if weather changes and leave time for the return before another appointment. Current guides, boats, facilities and access have not been inspected here. The photograph shows an earlier visit in 2017; it cannot answer those questions for today."
      }
    ],
