    "practical": [
      {
        "heading": "Know what a sighting can be",
        "text": "A glimpse may be all you see. The Chilika Development Authority describes Irrawaddy dolphins as shy and says that visitors rarely see the whole animal; part of the head or tail may appear at the surface. A boat booking cannot promise you a particular view. Ask the guide what to look for before everyone starts scanning the water. If you notice something, describe where it appeared using the boat’s direction or a landmark. Keep an uncertain glimpse uncertain; a photograph is not the only way to remember what you saw."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Ask how the boat will approach",
        "text": "The International Whaling Commission’s Chilika case study reports a difficult balance: dolphin tourism brings local income, while engine noise, propellers and crowding can disturb the animals. The way a boat approaches is part of the experience you are choosing. There is a concrete example in the same account. In 2005, training by the Chilika Development Authority and Whale and Dolphin Conservation taught operators to turn engines off near dolphins and use long poles in shallow water; propeller guards were also encouraged. That is a historical training example, not proof of how a particular boat operates today. Before booking, ask how the operator avoids chasing, surrounding or cutting across dolphins, and what they do if several boats gather. Ask to see the guidance they follow. Do not press the driver for a closer photograph, and support a decision to leave animals space."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Agree on the outing before boarding",
        "text": "Discuss the route, total charge, time on the water and return point together. Ask whether extra stops are included or charged separately, and how a shortened trip or cancellation would be handled. Settle those questions before setting out. Check boarding help, suitable lifejackets for everyone, shade and the length of time without a toilet stop. Explain any mobility or seating needs while you can still choose another arrangement. If the weather or boarding does not suit your group, ask about a shorter outing or time on shore."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Let the lagoon be enough",
        "text": "Choose one thing beyond the hoped-for dolphin photograph to pay attention to: the shoreline, the movement of boats or a bird you can watch without disturbing it. Ask the guide about something you actually notice, rather than expecting a performance or a prescribed story. Afterwards, separate what you saw from what someone explained and what you could not identify. Credit a guide’s account with their permission. A visit with no dolphin sighting can still leave you with a better understanding of the place; it need not end in a chase."
      }
