    "practical": [
      {
        "heading": "Begin with the shallows",
        "text": "Mangalajodi sits along the northern fringe of Chilika. Here, reed beds and shallow water provide places for birds to feed, shelter and breed. Think of the wetland as a collection of habitats: the open channel and the edge of the reeds offer different things to notice. Choose one bird to watch for a few minutes. Where does it stand? Does it search the surface or put its bill into the water? Ask your guide what those movements tell you. A name becomes easier to remember when you have something to connect it to."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What winter changes",
        "text": "Resident birds share this wetland with seasonal arrivals. November–February is the winter birdwatching period described by Incredible India, when migrants reach Chilika from regions including Central Asia. The season helps you plan; it cannot tell you which birds will be in front of you that morning. Keep a short record of the date, place and what you actually notice. If an identification is uncertain, leave it uncertain. A photograph or a few notes about shape and behaviour can become a useful question for a guide."
      },
      {
        "heading": "People are part of the picture",
        "text": "Odisha Tourism describes Mangalajodi’s change from a place associated with bird poaching to one known for community conservation and guiding. Incredible India also describes local involvement in bird monitoring and habitat restoration. These accounts explain why meeting a guide is part of understanding the place, beyond finding birds. Ask how the wetland has changed during the years your guide has known it, and what conservation work involves today. Listen for particular examples. The broad story does not mean that every resident had the same past or that every tourism business supports conservation in the same way."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Leave room for the birds",
        "text": "Watch from a distance that lets birds carry on feeding or resting. Avoid drawing them out with recorded calls, and give nests and roosts plenty of space. These suggestions follow the American Birding Association’s guidance on reducing disturbance; local access rules still apply. If you cannot get the photograph without moving closer, let the photograph go. Use binoculars if you have them, keep voices low and follow your guide’s advice about where to stop. The useful question is what the bird is doing while you watch."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Before setting out",
        "text": "Arrange the departure point, guide, duration and return transport directly. Ask about boarding, suitable lifejackets and the day’s weather and water conditions. If anyone in your group needs help with steps, balance or a seated break, describe that need before booking. Check drinking water, toilets and shade, too. Keep time free for watching instead of trying to fit several lagoon gateways into the same outing. Current guide availability, boat arrangements and facilities have not been confirmed for this guide."
      }
